@c constants.texi --- constants reference for the cmacs calculator.
@c Hand-written twin of doc_org/cmacs/calculator/constants.org --- keep in sync.
@node Calculator Constants
@section Constants

@cindex calculator constants
@cindex CODATA constants

Two kinds of constants are available in calculator expressions.  The
eight @emph{symbolic constants} below are bare names the validator
accepts without declaration; they stay exact symbols until numeric
evaluation folds them (the engine wraps every numeric evaluation in
@code{evalv()}, so a plain @code{pi} already prints as a float).  The
@emph{physical constants} are entries in GNU Calc's units table: they
are units, not numbers, and keep their dimensions through arithmetic.
Every example is real output from the engine.

@subsection Symbolic Constants

@table @code

@item pi
The circle constant, the ratio of a circle's circumference to its
diameter.  Kept exact in symbolic results; folded to a 12-digit float
by numeric evaluation.  The engine's corrected default is radians, so
@code{pi} is the natural argument for trigonometry.

Value: 3.14159265359

@example
pi  =>  3.14159265359
sin(pi/2)  =>  1.
@end example

@item e
Euler's number, the base of the natural logarithm.  Note the collision
with the units table: a bare @code{e} in an expression is always
Euler's number, while the @emph{elementary charge} is the unit
@code{ech}.

Value: 2.71828182846

@example
e  =>  2.71828182846
ln(e^2)  =>  2
e^(i*pi)  =>  -1.
@end example

@item i
The imaginary unit, sqrt(-1).  Complex results print in Calc's
rectangular notation @code{(re, im)}; @code{re}, @code{im},
@code{conj}, @code{arg}, @code{polar} and @code{rect} take them apart
and convert them.

Value: (0, 1)

@example
i^2  =>  -1
(2 + 3 i) * (2 - 3 i)  =>  13
@end example

@item phi
The golden ratio, (1 + sqrt(5))/2, satisfying phi^2 = phi + 1.

Value: 1.61803398875

@example
phi  =>  1.61803398875
phi^2 - phi  =>  1.
@end example

@item gamma
The Euler--Mascheroni constant, the limit of the difference between
the harmonic series and the natural logarithm.

Value: 0.577215664902

@example
gamma  =>  0.577215664902
exp(gamma)  =>  1.78107241799
@end example

@item inf
Positive infinity.  Arithmetic propagates it the way limits do: finite
values are absorbed, and indeterminate combinations collapse to
@code{nan}.  Negate it for negative infinity.

Value: inf

@example
1/inf  =>  0
inf + inf  =>  inf
inf - inf  =>  nan
@end example

@item uinf
Unsigned (directionless) infinity: infinite magnitude with unknown
sign or direction, as in a pole of a function.  @code{abs} of any
infinity is a plain @code{inf}.

Value: uinf

@example
uinf + 5  =>  uinf
abs(uinf)  =>  inf
@end example

@item nan
Not-a-number, the result of an indeterminate operation.  It absorbs
every operation it touches, including comparisons like @code{max}.

Value: nan

@example
nan + 1  =>  nan
max(nan, 5)  =>  nan
@end example

@end table

@subsection Physical Constants (CODATA)

GNU Calc's units table carries the CODATA physical constants, and the
calculator resolves any unit name in an expression, so @code{c},
@code{G}, @code{h} and friends are all available --- as @emph{units
with dimensions}, never as bare numbers (@pxref{Calculator Families}).
@code{2 c} stays @code{2 c} until you convert it; conversion is what
produces digits:

@table @code
@item cmacs-calculator-convert-units
Convert an expression to named units ---
@code{("1 c" "m/s")} @result{} @code{299792458}.
@item cmacs-calculator-to-base-units
Reduce to base SI --- @code{"1 c"} @result{} @code{299792458 m / s}.
@end table

Interactively the same pair is @kbd{M-x cmacs-calculator-convert}
(empty target units mean base SI), and MCP exposes it as
@code{calc_convert_units}.

Two naming traps.  First, @code{e} in an expression is Euler's number,
not the elementary charge --- use @code{ech}.  Second, @code{h} is the
@emph{Planck constant}, not the hour (that is @code{hr}):
@code{km/h} silently means kilometres per Planck constant.

@multitable @columnfractions 0.12 0.38 0.5
@headitem Unit @tab Value @tab Constant
@item c     @tab 299792458 m/s             @tab Speed of light
@item h     @tab 6.62607015e-34 J s        @tab Planck constant
@item hbar  @tab h / (2 pi)                @tab Reduced Planck constant
@item ech   @tab 1.602176634e-19 C         @tab Elementary charge
@item k     @tab 1.380649e-23 J/K          @tab Boltzmann constant
@item Nav   @tab 6.02214076e23 / mol       @tab Avogadro constant
@item eps0  @tab ech^2 / (2 alpha h c)     @tab Permittivity of vacuum
@item mu0   @tab 1 / (eps0 c^2)            @tab Permeability of vacuum
@item sigma @tab 2 pi^5 k^4 / (15 h^3 c^2) @tab Stefan--Boltzmann constant
@item alpha @tab 7.2973525643e-3           @tab Fine structure constant
@item Ryd   @tab 10973731.568157 /m        @tab Rydberg constant
@item me    @tab 9.1093837139e-31 kg       @tab Electron rest mass
@item mp    @tab 1.67262192595e-27 kg      @tab Proton rest mass
@item mn    @tab 1.67492750056e-27 kg      @tab Neutron rest mass
@item mmu   @tab 1.883531627e-28 kg        @tab Muon rest mass
@item muB   @tab 9.2740100657e-24 J/T      @tab Bohr magneton
@item muN   @tab 5.0507837393e-27 J/T      @tab Nuclear magneton
@item mue   @tab -9.2847646917e-24 J/T     @tab Electron magnetic moment
